WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.660
Are Millennials more prone to burnout than other generations?

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.200
The answer is maybe 
but not because they're Millennials.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.500
It's not some biological thing that

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.559
an entire generation is more prone to burnout than a previous generation.

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:15.073
I don't think that's it at all

00:00:15.073 --> 00:00:16.900
but I think that the trend of how we live our lives is very different.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.800
I think we are more solitary in how we live our lives.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.120
And there's good data that there's fewer marriages,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.680
there's fewer people living together,
people are staying single longer

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.862
and even the quality of relationships

00:00:31.862 --> 00:00:33.840
which I've talked about

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.669
and others have talked about as well

00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:37.360
where social media and cell phones sometimes interrupt and get in the way

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.740
and frustrate our ability to form those
deep meaningful relationships.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.760
Millennials just by virtue of when they were born

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.520
and what technologies were available that day 
were sort of on the front line

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:56.129
the first ones to grow up in this world 
and these trends.

00:00:56.129 --> 00:01:01.630
And so I think that the opportunity to ask for help

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:08.180
or accept help when it's offered,

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.158
to express that kind of vulnerability

00:01:10.158 --> 00:01:11.886
to deal with that kind of stress.

00:01:11.886 --> 00:01:14.400
I mean think about how easy it is when there's a  conflict with another human being.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.500
It's just much easier to ghost them than to have a confrontation, right?

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.980
You've been dating somebody for a while, you're really not into it anymore.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.820
Instead of gaining closure and having a conversation,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:24.320
even a fight,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.799
we just disappear.

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:26.299
Why?

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:29.579
Because it's easier. Let's be honest.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:31.320
But I think that actually not only hurts the
other person

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.220
it actually creates loneliness for us.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.500
Sometimes those confrontations, even if they're difficult, are valuable.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.334
There's some good data on this—

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:40.360
that somebody who gets ignored at work,

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.601
when their boss never talks to them,

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:44.220
they never get any kind of positive feedback, 
never kind of negative feedback.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.299
I can't remember exact numbers but

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:48.799
it's something like seven percent are engaged in their work.

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:53.080
It's an overwhelmingly high number of people are disengaged

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.900
simply because they get no positive or negative feedback

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.500
and if they were to get in trouble,
their level of engagement actually goes up.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.540
And if we get one piece of positive feedback from our bosses

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.289
our level of engagement quadruples or something like that.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:11.074
In other words

00:02:11.074 --> 00:02:12.980
getting negative feedback actually

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.190
makes us more engaged in our work than
getting no feedback

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:20.034
because at least somebody knows we exist and that's the point.

00:02:20.034 --> 00:02:21.440
I think one of the reasons that this generation

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.013
and maybe younger generations

00:02:24.013 --> 00:02:25.620
and again not as a result of the generation thing

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.180
but rather the the life in which they've grown up
just by virtue of when they grow up

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.160
probably does make them more prone to burnout.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.980
Probably because they find themselves
doing more things themselves.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:42.500
That the concept of partnership or help is reduced.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.290
So when you have more to do,
when you have more on your plate,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.663
you have to be the breadwinner, 
the homemaker ... all of this,

00:02:48.663 --> 00:02:50.500
of course we're going to burn out more.

